From 2e3dc5e1a8db954ef4c5616a1677527c2a9cfc27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pieroviano <piero.viano@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 05:03:33 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Gtk4: inline @import so a theme's encoding cannot cost it
 rules

Two buttons on the sample's Test1 lost the icons its stylesheet gives them, while
every other rule in the same file applied -- which made it look like an image
problem. It is an encoding problem.

theme/Test1.css is GBK-encoded, and the first byte that is not valid UTF-8 sits
in the comment immediately above #Test1 #button16. Gtk4's CSS parser requires
valid UTF-8 and abandons the rules around such a byte; it reports nothing at all,
so #button16, #button17 and their :hover rule simply did not exist. Dumping the
provider shows them missing while every image in the surviving rules resolved
fine. Gtk3's parser did not validate, which is why the file has worked for years.

The library already reads the top-level stylesheet itself, so style.css survives
this -- .NET's decoder substitutes rather than failing. An @import was left for
Gtk to follow, and Gtk then opens that file raw. So follow imports here instead:
inline the imported sheet, decoded tolerantly (strict UTF-8 first, otherwise
Latin-1, which cannot fail and maps every byte to a character, so a bad byte
costs at most the comment it sits in and never a rule), with its own relative
references resolved against its own directory -- which is what Gtk would have
used, having opened the file there. Inlining also keeps the imported rules in the
cascade position the import had.

Generic, not keyed to the sample: any user theme that Gtk3 accepted keeps
working. An import that cannot be resolved, or that carries a scheme, is left
exactly as before, and nesting is depth-capped so mutually importing sheets
terminate.

The test's fixture uses the exact byte run from the real file. A hand-rolled
substitute is not good enough -- Gtk tolerates some invalid sequences and not
others, and my first approximation passed while proving nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
